ry routes that include oral medications, injections, intravenous infusions, and (for certain bladder cancers) intravesical administration. [Data: Entities (491); Relationships (700)]\\n\\nThe community’s relationship structure reinforces this centrality: CHEMOTHERAPY connects outward to administration approaches (intravenous administration; infusion pump delivery), regimen constructs (single-agent and combination regimens), example agents, and clinical decision concepts such as regimen length and disease-stage guidance. [Data: Relationships (700,14219,8258,8259,9519,+more)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Regimen design is a major organizing axis: single-agent therapy vs combination/multi-agent regimens, including multi-agent use in AML.\",\"explanation\":\"The data explicitly distinguishes SINGLE AGENT THERAPY (one drug) from COMBINATION OR MULTI-AGENT REGIMEN (two or more drugs), framing regimen structure as a foundational decision point in chemotherapy planning. [Data: Entities (4005,4006); Relationships (8258,8259)]\\n\\nMulti-agent chemotherapy is specifically described as common in AML, with combined agents working differently and causing different side effects—highlighting how regimen complexity can expand both therapeutic mechanisms and toxicity profiles. [Data: Entities (2998,1925); Relationships (5814,3251)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community enumerates representative chemotherapy drugs and ties some to drug classes (anthracyclines, antimetabolites).\",\"explanation\":\"Several specific agents are listed as examples within the broader chemotherapy category, including cisplatin, cyclophosphamide, docetaxel, eribulin, paclitaxel (Taxol/Abraxane), and vinorelbine. This positions the community as a taxonomy-like mapping from the umbrella concept (chemotherapy) to concrete drugs. [Data: Entities (3950,3951,3952,3965,3967,+more); Relationships (8214,8215,8216,8251,8254,+more)]\\n\\nThe network further classifies some drugs into pharmacologic classes: doxorubicin, liposomal doxor