ties (491,4272,5999,6161); Relationships (13658,14098)]\\n\\nThe relationships explicitly connect chemotherapy to affecting the WHOLE BODY and to harming NORMAL HEALTHY CELLS, making systemic exposure and off-target toxicity a key theme in the community’s risk profile. [Data: Relationships (13658,14098)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Documented side-effect and safety considerations include acute symptoms and long-term harms, plus a pregnancy-related caution tied to birth defects.\",\"explanation\":\"Common/possible side effects named in the network include tingling or numbness and rash, indicating recognized symptom burdens that can arise during chemotherapy. [Data: Entities (5983,5984); Relationships (13655,13656)]\\n\\nLong-term effects are also explicitly listed: chemotherapy can be associated with later development of other cancers and with infertility (inability to have children), highlighting durable impact beyond the treatment window. [Data: Entities (5095,5096); Relationships (11188,11190)]\\n\\nA pregnancy-related safety caution is captured via BIRTH DEFECTS linked to PREGNANCY, where the text advises avoiding pregnancy/impregnation during chemotherapy due to birth-defect risk. [Data: Entities (4293); Relationships (8997)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Clinical-context guidance includes adjuvant/after-surgery chemotherapy, post-chemoradiation treatment for lingering cells, and colon-cancer regimen/stage references (CAPEOX; stage 2A/2B/2C; pMMR/MSS).\",\"explanation\":\"Primary or adjuvant chemotherapy is defined as chemotherapy given after surgery, and chemotherapy is also described as potentially used after successful chemoradiation to kill lingering cancer cells—illustrating sequencing roles (post-surgery and consolidation contexts). [Data: Entities (2270,2811); Relationships (3932,5181)]\\n\\nFor colon-cancer-related scenarios, chemotherapy is linked to pMMR/MSS cancers when systemic therapy is planned before surgery, and CAPEOX is listed as a recommended regimen for high-risk 